WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:03.400
Hello everybody, I'm Luca from Luca Lampariello.com

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.210
and this time not only am I speaking Spanish, 
but I'm with a very special person by the name of Pablo.

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:15.150
Pablo, how are you? Hello Luca! How are you? 
Very well! Very well in Rome. I don't know if it's hot

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:19.930
in...where are you now, in Barcelona?
I'm in Thailand, so you can imagine how hot it is.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:26.960
Yes, I can imagine!
Then, Pablo, could you introduce yourself to
my audience? Maybe, I don't know, some of them surely

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:39.380
already know you, but perhaps others do not.  So could you tell me about what you do in life? And also, I know you're a polyglot

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:47.130
so you can tell me a little about your experience as a polyglot? 
Yes, well, I am the creator of the YouTube channel

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:54.710
Dreaming Spanish, for people who are learning Spanish, and well, at one point in my life, a

00:00:54.710 --> 00:01:01.082
few years ago I realized that you could learn
languages in a much better way, and then later

00:01:01.082 --> 00:01:04.648
after a couple
of failed projects, of failed attempts to help

00:01:04.648 --> 00:01:09.318
people I realized that is that there was a
need for comprehensible content

00:01:09.318 --> 00:01:17.616
That there is no content easy enough to understand for
someone to start learning a language from zero

00:01:17.616 --> 00:01:23.047
In the way that it works, in the way that science, that research

00:01:23.047 --> 00:01:31.053
tells us that we really acquire vocabulary,
we acquire grammar, so after having learned

00:01:31.053 --> 00:01:38.115
several languages, having learned English,
French, and Japanese, I decided to apply what

00:01:38.115 --> 00:01:44.499
I had learned to help other people and I started
with Spanish because it is the language I

00:01:44.499 --> 00:01:48.829
speak as a native speaker, but in the future
I would also like to expand

00:01:48.829 --> 00:01:54.219
And to do and apply the same concept but in
other languages. Very well, and what is this

00:01:54.219 --> 00:01:59.079
massive input? I mean, not massive input...comprehensible
input, and then we can talk about massive input

00:01:59.079 --> 00:02:06.929
so could you give me a concrete definition 
or a concrete example of what comprehensible input is

00:02:06.929 --> 00:02:10.950
because Stephen Krashen who is a famous
linguist who introduced this concept, but

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:18.339
concretely, for example, if you...what would you
advise a person who starts speaking Spanish

00:02:18.339 --> 00:02:28.157
What kind of material would he have to choose? 
And then, a person who already has, let's say, an intermediate base in Spanish

00:02:28.157 --> 00:02:33.082
How would you advise them and how does the definition
of comprehensible input change according to

00:02:33.082 --> 00:02:39.531
the level you have in a foreign language?
Well, comprehensible input

00:02:39.531 --> 00:02:45.920
is two words. One is input, and you have to
understand that input is something you hear

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.893
or read. You have to be listening or reading. Speaking

00:02:50.893 --> 00:02:57.339
and doing exercises is not input, 
that's the first thing. And the second thing

00:02:57.339 --> 00:03:02.440
is that it is comprehensible; you understand
it. Understanding it does not mean you understand

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:11.285
the logical reasoning, that you understand the rules
of grammar. It means you understand the message

00:03:11.285 --> 00:03:17.806
that is being communicated by what you are
hearing or what you are reading, so once you

00:03:17.806 --> 00:03:27.040
understand what that person is trying to communicate
is when you start to associate those words

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:32.219
you are hearing or reading with the meaning
thanks to the knowledge you already have or

00:03:32.219 --> 00:03:40.836
the context or the rest of the words in that
text or in that video you are watching, so

00:03:40.836 --> 00:03:46.932
what you are saying about what it means at
each level, is that this idea that I

00:03:46.932 --> 00:03:52.767
just explained can be applied to all levels,
what happens is that when you start from scratch

00:03:52.767 --> 00:03:58.019
if someone starts talking to you, all the
words, if you start from scratch, you don't

00:03:58.019 --> 00:04:03.740
know any of the words, right? Whatever happens, there are going to be a lot of words

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:08.433
that you are not really going to
understand exactly what they are referring

00:04:08.433 --> 00:04:15.523
to and when you start when you start learning
the language it's more like linking together, little

00:04:15.523 --> 00:04:22.983
by little, all the words that contain the most meaning,
that have the most important meaning, and you

00:04:22.983 --> 00:04:27.770
need something that is very easy to understand,
you really need to be talking about something

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:33.841
that you are seeing, that they are showing
you a cup, and they tell you "cup", something that is really

00:04:33.841 --> 00:04:42.404
very obvious, right? Then, yeah, at the lower levels
it's mostly this, that you are noticing

00:04:42.404 --> 00:04:47.777
that there are certain words that are used
in certain situations, and you start to link  them

00:04:47.777 --> 00:04:55.465
at higher levels and especially when you start reading, the way
you can acquire new words, is because you know

00:04:55.465 --> 00:05:06.080
enough of the context you know enough of the
other words that appear in that text, for example

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:11.602
and in this case, the case of learning vocabulary
and grammar by reading,  the research that has

00:05:11.602 --> 00:05:19.459
been done so far points out that the maximum
speed of learning by reading is when you already

00:05:19.459 --> 00:05:28.738
understand or already know 98% of the words
that appear in the text. Or rather, only one

00:05:28.738 --> 00:05:33.916
word out of 50 that you do not know. This is
a much easier kind of reading

00:05:33.916 --> 00:05:40.361
than most people think of. Many people think that
it has to be difficult, that if it is not

00:05:40.361 --> 00:05:44.367
difficult what you are doing, you 
are not learning, right?

00:05:44.367 --> 00:05:51.531
Very well explained, I think
that for me the comprehensible input is

00:05:51.531 --> 00:05:56.864
input that you can understand, that you are
in the middle of understanding, like for example videos where there are

00:05:56.864 --> 00:06:03.220
subtitles. Nowadays, it's possible to watch videos&nbsp;
read videos in two languages

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:10.060
not only, for example, for your videos in Spanish, but there are tools that allow you to

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:15.070
translate directly, to have a translation&nbsp;
this, to me, is comprehensible input in the sense that you can

00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:21.130
see, understand, listen, and also&nbsp;
directly read, a text with audio, something

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:26.170
that you can read and listen, and also, maybe, you could translate it&nbsp;
so that you have the translation

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:33.280
this is comprehensible input, but also for me it's important and I think in this sense&nbsp;
I think you do

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:40.985
a hell of a job, the sense of comprehensible input is important, it's&nbsp;
it's necessary but it's not enough

00:06:40.985 --> 00:06:46.160
because the most important thing is also&nbsp;
that this comprehensible input is something that you like

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:52.000
we are used to in school to do things that we don't like, 
you remember in school.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:59.500
that they gave us things we didn't care about at all, and&nbsp;
now we live in this information age

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:06.190
where we can choose things, like
what I like about your website, you have

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:11.170
a site and a YouTube channel, we're going to&nbsp;
talk about that at the end, but in general what I

00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:18.790
like is that you've, let's say, you've created a system&nbsp;
where you have content for "Super Beginners", I

00:07:18.790 --> 00:07:26.530
like the concept of "Super Beginner", "Beginner", so&nbsp;
you have various possibilities for it to be

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:31.780
very diversified, and you have possibilities&nbsp;
for beginners, those who have an intermediate

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:37.840
level and even those with advanced level,&nbsp;
and if I'm not mistaken, you also have, for example,

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:44.320
history lessons, little things about history,
interesting content. I was wondering

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:51.755
how you got the idea to create this site,
and why you created this site.

00:07:51.755 --> 00:07:59.050
Well, the idea of teaching a language without teaching the language,&nbsp;
but teaching something else, but using the language

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:06.490
well, it's something that's been used in other situations as well,&nbsp;
but mostly the method

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:14.680
that I use is based on a school in Bangkok&nbsp;
where Thai is taught, and in fact I spent

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:21.070
one year in this school learning Thai&nbsp;
myself, because the method they use fit

00:08:21.070 --> 00:08:28.330
very much with my experiences when I learned&nbsp;
English and Japanese, and I wanted to try it. For me it was a

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:35.370
experiment, I came here one year and I wanted to see how well 
I learned Thai, and it worked very well for me.

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:42.870
so I decided to adapt the method they use in this&nbsp;
this school to the online world so that millions of

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:49.890
of people can benefit, not only the 30 people that fit in a class, right?

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:57.480
Great! And we talked for a second about massive input,&nbsp;
comprehensible input, we said comprehensible and

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:05.340
And interesting input, it's super important, but if&nbsp;
someone "old school" asks you

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:11.790
"Well, how can you learn a language without learning&nbsp;
grammar?" What would you say to him? 

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:20.160
For example, he says  "Okay, comprehensible input, but I don't understand&nbsp;the subjunctive, or how it's used"

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:25.740
I know that English speakers, and foreigners in general&nbsp;
have a lot of problems with the subjunctive

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:32.220
Italians have a little less trouble with the subjunctive, because it's a
very similar structure in the two languages, but

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:37.440
How do you respond to a person who says "No, no,&nbsp;
I like grammar, I'm used to learning

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:45.500
grammar, how could you teach me grammar without&nbsp;
teaching it to me?" Well, how I

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:53.480
explain it is that at no time has it been shown&nbsp;
that rationally learning the

00:09:53.480 --> 00:10:00.680
rules of grammar is useful for acquiring&nbsp;
grammar, useful for understanding it in a way...

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:11.840
Like intuition...an intuitive way and that you can use it in&nbsp;
conversation without having to think about the

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:17.570
structures that you're using, and without having to think about&nbsp;
thinking about the grammar, and having to stop to

00:10:17.570 --> 00:10:26.990
make sure you're saying it right, and yet what the research does show

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:33.800
that's been done so far, is that adults can continue to learn how&nbsp;children learn.

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:40.250
Children learn the grammar of their native language, 
they learn to use it without&nbsp;anyone having

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:47.270
explained the rules to them. Children, when they start school at the age of six, they already speak their own language.

00:10:47.270 --> 00:10:52.670
They are already native speakers of the language, and up to that point&nbsp;, they've never been taught grammar before.

00:10:53.630 --> 00:11:00.290
And my personal experience is that I, too, have&nbsp;
learned more than one language in this way, without

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:07.580
ever having learned the rules of grammar,
but still I have the "feeling", I have the intuition of

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:14.420
how to use what is correct, and what is not correct. 
Very well, and it is&nbsp;true. This is based

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:22.100
in science. For example, I always, let's say, I always had&nbsp;
this intuition...we have this intuition, people that learn

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:26.930
languages, we realize that the brain learns, 
there's a "brain-friendly" way,

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:32.720
as they say in English, and a way that&nbsp;
is not very "brain-friendly", that doesn't adapt much

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:37.790
to the way our brain learns, and&nbsp;
one of the things that they've discovered is that

00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:45.110
grammar rules, if you want to deliberately teach&nbsp;
the rules of grammar, this doesn't

00:11:45.110 --> 00:11:52.160
work, because in our brain there are two parts&nbsp;
that are dedicated to...one is dedicated to so-called

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:57.290
"declarative memory" and the other is...I think in&nbsp;
I think in Spanish it's called "procedimental" (procedural) I thought

00:11:57.290 --> 00:12:02.600
The Spanish word was "procedural", but&nbsp;I looked it up in Wikipedia
and it's "procedimental", and declarative memory has

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:08.240
to do with the facts, with the things that you know, with&nbsp;
the rules of grammar that you know, a separate thing,

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:14.870
procedural memory is what we know how to do, or&nbsp;
procedures. Speaking a language, our own

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:21.450
native language, has to do with&nbsp;
procedural memory that processes, let's say,

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:27.300
the way we work with information, and say one
word after the other, and we're just

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:32.040
sabotaging ourselves, when at school we learn in a way
that's not very natural, so

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:37.050
I'm convinced, like you, that you learn a
foreign language the way you learn your native language

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:41.820
there are many other linguists, there are two&nbsp;
categories of linguists those who believe that it is

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:47.910
the same process, and those who don't, but
other than that, I'm with you, I agree with you about the

00:12:47.910 --> 00:12:54.090
fact that you can learn grammar in a way that is,&nbsp;
let's say, indirect and a little bit direct, let's say, 

00:12:54.090 --> 00:13:00.630
it depends on the language, but fundamentally, one learns... 
It's better to learn, let's say, 

00:13:00.630 --> 00:13:05.790
grammar through massive input, because our brain
learns so much in this way

00:13:05.790 --> 00:13:11.400
Okay, we've talked a little bit about grammar through massive input, 
Let's talk a little about...you teach Spanish

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:17.340
so you have a lot of experience with what they ask you,
with what your audience asks you.

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:25.110
What are the biggest linguistic difficulties or obstacles&nbsp;
that one of the...

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:32.310
of the English-speaking world and foreigners in&nbsp;general?
And the beliefs...you know, when talking about

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:37.110
language learning, there are people who say&nbsp;
you have to move to a new country to learn a new language.

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:46.620
and, I don't know, so (what are) the obstacles, 
the difficulties, and beliefs in general?

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:56.790
So yeah, these are all preconceptions based on  
traditional language teaching, right? And a lot of things

00:13:56.790 --> 00:14:04.530
about the way languages are taught are 
based on how dead languages were taught,

00:14:04.530 --> 00:14:12.240
the way Latin and Ancient Greek were taught, and 
many other things are based on

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:21.780
in Skinner's Behaviorism, on the fact that 
by doing one thing and repeating it, you get used to doing it

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:26.550
this way, and then you're always going to do it this way, and 
and that's why we think we have to

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:34.020
practice, we have to do drills until we get it right,
that they have to correct us, we have to have these

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:42.730
good and bad experiences, and learn 
from the good ones and so on, right? Meanwhile this has

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:49.540
never been shown to work for languages. So 
it comes from there. That's why people think that they have

00:14:49.540 --> 00:14:55.240
they have to learn the rules, they have to understand 
why you use this tense, when you use this verb tense

00:14:55.240 --> 00:15:02.840
and when you use another, how, I don't know,
in Spanish, you use "Se" ("if"),

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:11.430
the subjunctive mood, the different types of past tense,
a lot of people think that this is necessary.

00:15:11.430 --> 00:15:18.660
People want to know the translation for 
the words, for them, they want to know how

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:24.810
this word is said in their language. But then, 
often, the words either don't have a translation

00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:32.790
or when you translate them, you're actually learning things
that are not true, because a lot of times, even though

00:15:32.790 --> 00:15:39.690
they are words that come from the same origin, 
they cover different ranges of meaning, right?

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:49.159
And by learning them as translations, I think you
can also end up having an incorrect idea

00:15:49.159 --> 00:15:52.840
of the meaning and the situations in which
those words can be used.

00:15:55.790 --> 00:16:00.950
Yes, normally, when people ask me, 
or when people don't understand something about

00:16:00.950 --> 00:16:05.030
way we teach, it's because of this, because
they have these preconceptions

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:14.430
of the traditional method of teaching languages.
And difficulties? So with

00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:19.590
difficulties, has it happened to you, 
for example that you met these

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:27.870
people who demotivate themselves? And how do you do it, 
motivating yourself as a polyglot, and how do you 

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:33.690
teach others how to motivate themselves? Because I think 
it's possible to motivate someone, but it's 

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:38.070
not going to last a long time. 
People always talk about motivation, because

00:16:38.070 --> 00:16:45.780
I think, at the end of the day, the ones who get there, who
speak a language fluently are those who

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:53.250
do it, learn it, in a long...let's say as 
a long term project, so regarding motivation

00:16:53.250 --> 00:17:00.930
What do you suggest to people when they get 
demotivated, and they think this is too hard?

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:08.130
because I noticed that the ones that get demotivated the most are 
also those who have never learned to...

00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:15.230
have never learned a language fluently, and so 
they're not sure that they're going to get it.

00:17:15.230 --> 00:17:23.450
Well, yeah, motivation is a huge issue, isn't it?
So, motivation, everything that applies as far as

00:17:23.450 --> 00:17:28.580
to motivation in general, to any goal or
objective that you want to achieve

00:17:28.580 --> 00:17:34.580
also applies to languages. The fact of showing
people a clear path that they have to follow

00:17:34.580 --> 00:17:42.740
in order to get there, right? The fact that people believe in 
you, believe that you know what you're talking about. 

00:17:42.740 --> 00:17:49.430
It's like you say, if the person has had experience with
learning a language, they already know that they can do it.

00:17:49.430 --> 00:17:56.120
It's going to be much more difficult for them to lose 
motivation, because they already believe in themselves

00:17:56.120 --> 00:18:03.560
If they've never done it, one way you can help them 
is by showing them examples of other people

00:18:03.560 --> 00:18:10.670
of people as similar to them as possible. 
They may come from the same country, they may come from

00:18:10.670 --> 00:18:16.730
the same social class and everything, well, they will believe you 
much more and they're going to be more motivated. All these are

00:18:16.730 --> 00:18:22.370
things that apply to motivation in general, right?
For any goal that you want to achieve.

00:18:22.370 --> 00:18:30.900
The fact that you're seeing progress because of this. but then 
there are things that apply specifically to

00:18:30.900 --> 00:18:41.340
languages, and in the case of languages,
I think it's important to set students up with the

00:18:41.340 --> 00:18:49.140
right expectations. And this is something that I think 
traditional language teaching doesn't do very well.

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:55.050
Because when you go to a language class,
they give you a vocabulary list. They teach you some

00:18:55.050 --> 00:19:01.080
grammar points. You go home, and you think okay,
look, I've come this far, we've finished

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:08.160
topic one of the book, and you've made all this progress. 
And then you finish course 2, or 3, or whatever it is that

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:14.430
the school offers, and when people finish all
the courses in a language school, they still can't 

00:19:14.430 --> 00:19:20.340
speak fluently. But they've already finished all the subject matter, 
and they've finished all the grammar

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:27.090
that is easy to explain, that is easy to go over, and 
to check on a test that they have learned it

00:19:27.090 --> 00:19:34.410
and I think that this is what produces what they call 
"the plateau", the place where a lot of people stay.

00:19:34.410 --> 00:19:42.150
and then they feel like they're not moving forward. I think it's because of this, because they've been using the

00:19:42.150 --> 00:19:49.350
rational part of their brain to learn 
the rules of grammar, and this has given them some

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:54.480
very high expectations. They believe that the speed of 
learning should be this fast, and 

00:19:54.480 --> 00:19:59.760
they have to keep learning at this speed. While 
the speed at which they have been really acquiring

00:19:59.760 --> 00:20:08.130
language, at which their subconscious, or the more intuitive part 
of the brain has been learning, has been

00:20:08.130 --> 00:20:12.570
slower and it's going to continue to be slower, because
that's the actual speed at which they're learning language.

00:20:12.570 --> 00:20:20.760
And I think setting correct expectations 
from the beginning, that this is going to be ongoing, 

00:20:20.760 --> 00:20:27.210
that acquiring language is going to happen little by little, 
that you're not even going to completely learn one 

00:20:27.210 --> 00:20:35.340
word today. Today, maybe you'll be able to associate it with 
one example where you've heard it, when you've heard it in

00:20:35.340 --> 00:20:42.390
a situation where it was referring to a specific thing.  
But today, you've learned 1% of this word

00:20:42.390 --> 00:20:48.240
you are going to have to hear it many more times, right?
Then I think it's important to set

00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:55.230
these expectations so that people won't be disappointed 
and think that they're not making progress.

00:20:55.230 --> 00:21:01.260
Very good answer! I always tell my 
students when they ask me, for example

00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:11.167
They ask me all the time: "When I am going to get a B2 or a C1?" and they are worried about goals.

00:21:11.167 --> 00:21:13.620
I always tell them to concentrate on the system, on

00:21:13.620 --> 00:21:19.800
habits, because the truth is that speaking 
a language is a marathon. If you want to speak a language

00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:27.014
language well, with fluency, and know the nuances of 
the culture and all of that, this takes years.

00:21:27.014 --> 00:21:33.270
It's going to take you years. And if you already know 
that you've got a long road ahead of you, 

00:21:33.270 --> 00:21:40.390
you're going to relax and you're going to enjoy the journey. 
So that's why I say if you worry, it's very important to set

00:21:40.390 --> 00:21:47.290
expectations, to clearly explain that it's going 
to take years. But you can make progress, and this

00:21:47.290 --> 00:21:56.140
doesn't mean that you can't talk, that you have to 
wait years before you can talk. You can talk!

00:21:56.140 --> 00:22:02.380
But you have to concentrate on the journey. One 
last question...I could clearly talk for hours

00:22:02.380 --> 00:22:15.018
but for obvious reasons, we have to come to the end 
of this conversation, this very interesting chat

00:22:15.018 --> 00:22:20.255
(The question is) to speak from the beginning, or wait (to speak)?

00:22:20.255 --> 00:22:28.600
That is, if, for example someone starts 
learning Spanish today, how would you advise them? To start

00:22:28.600 --> 00:22:34.900
from zero? To speak right away, because the 
sooner, the better? Or to wait for months or

00:22:34.900 --> 00:22:43.240
years before talking, because if you get more input
then it's going to be easier afterwards. Which school of thought

00:22:43.240 --> 00:22:54.020
do you belong to? Waiting, waiting, of course. Yes, 
based on the science that there is about it, 

00:22:54.020 --> 00:23:00.020
there's actually not much and there's less of it than we need. 
I think that more research needs to be done on the subject, but

00:23:00.020 --> 00:23:08.690
there are some articles that show that people 
who write less, that talk less, they get

00:23:08.690 --> 00:23:14.810
better scores on tests that actually measure 
language acquisition. Then there are people

00:23:15.710 --> 00:23:24.920
who are introverted, and who we can assume 
speak less, obtain better accents in

00:23:24.920 --> 00:23:31.280
the language they are learning than extroverted 
people, right? And this is something that hasn't been

00:23:31.280 --> 00:23:36.710
100% proven, and in the future we need 
to have more studies done. 

00:23:38.780 --> 00:23:47.621
And, well, another reason is that in general, I accept that 
to learn a language well, (you must) do it the way a child does it

00:23:48.020 --> 00:23:54.200
Then, if we have some reason to change something, if we believe that something can be improved, then we can see if it

00:23:54.200 --> 00:24:02.300
can be improved. But by default, kids do it well
and reach a very good final result.

00:24:02.300 --> 00:24:09.500
And children have what we call the "Silent Period". 
the period in which they don't talk, which happens both in

00:24:09.500 --> 00:24:17.930
babies, who don't use a lot of words that 
they can already understand, and it also happens that

00:24:17.930 --> 00:24:25.130
children who move away, who go to live in another country, 
they usually go for months without saying

00:24:25.130 --> 00:24:32.819
anything in the language that is spoken in that place,  and 
then one day, all of a sudden, they start to say things, and they start to talk, right? 

00:24:33.490 --> 00:24:39.030
This fits in very much also with Krashen's 
theory, that in the end it's the input, it's 

00:24:39.030 --> 00:24:47.090
what we receive, and not what we say. 
And there's also a practical part to this.

00:24:47.090 --> 00:24:56.030
To try talking when you don't know how...it's painful, it's difficult. 
And, well, for a lot of people it's not an agreeable experience

00:24:56.030 --> 00:25:02.660
But the more input you have received,
the more you've acquired, the easier it's going to be

00:25:02.660 --> 00:25:09.320
to talk, and begin to talk, right?
I more or less agree with you, I've also tried

00:25:09.320 --> 00:25:15.980
to talk a little earlier, but I always wait 
months before speaking, and that fits a lot

00:25:15.980 --> 00:25:21.050
with my experience, with yours, and with Krashen's. 
But well if a person wants to talk earlier

00:25:21.050 --> 00:25:28.520
because, I don't know, they need to for work, 
well, everyone is different, everyone has

00:25:28.520 --> 00:25:36.770
different existential circumstances, and well, the world's an 
amazing place because there's a bit of everything.

00:25:36.770 --> 00:25:41.420
Yes, yes, of course, in practice you have to 
choose, to see what are your values, what

00:25:41.420 --> 00:25:45.210
is the most important, and do what 
is going to be best for you. Of course!

00:25:45.210 --> 00:25:53.400
Pablo, it's been a blast, thank you very much. 
A blast to speak with you, a pleasure, and could you tell our

00:25:53.400 --> 00:25:59.880
viewers where they can find you, and then afterwards, I will 
put the links in the description box as well.

00:26:01.080 --> 00:26:07.230
Yes, come to the YouTube channel "Dreaming Spanish" 
where we have content for all levels

00:26:08.100 --> 00:26:13.320
like Luca said, we teach history, there are 
videos where I play video games,

00:26:13.320 --> 00:26:19.800
we have Latin American teachers too, 
and then we are working on our website

00:26:19.800 --> 00:26:25.680
that in the future we want to turn into 
a learning platform where you can really

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:33.350
learn starting from scratch and get to
basically any level, to at least a

00:26:33.350 --> 00:26:39.530
level that, if you'd prefer, I don't know, to go watch Netflix, 
go watch movies, go talk to people on

00:26:39.530 --> 00:26:49.040
the language, then we can to take you up to that level. And yeah, 
that's it. Phenomenal! Thank you very much, Pablo and

00:26:49.040 --> 00:26:54.868
have a great time in Thailand! Thanks to you for inviting me. 
See you soon, ciao!

